from common import unit,rule
unit('Arte, relatos y reseñas','Stories worth discussing','🎭','Describir una obra, interpretar su efecto y escribir una reseña útil sin reducirla a me gusta o no me gusta.',[
rule('Contar sin perder el hilo','En una reseña puedes usar presente para resumir el argumento de una obra y pasado para contar tu experiencia al verla. Mantén claro ese cambio de perspectiva. Describe solo los hechos necesarios para justificar tu valoración y evita revelar el desenlace cuando el lector busca una recomendación. Una narración oral necesita orientación, desarrollo y resultado; selecciona detalles que permitan seguir la historia.','The story follows…; When I watched it…; The turning point comes when…','El presente narrativo no es un error si se mantiene una perspectiva coherente.',
'The story {follows} a family who return to their village.|following|is follow',
'By the time I reached the final chapter, I {had changed} my opinion of the narrator.|have changed|will change',
'While the main character searches for an answer, the reader notices several clues.'),
rule('Evaluar con criterios y ejemplos','-ed describe a menudo una reacción; -ing describe lo que la provoca: bored frente a boring. Compelling, predictable y thought-provoking precisan una valoración, pero necesitan un ejemplo. La opinión puede referirse a ritmo, personajes, estilo o público adecuado. Reconoce una limitación que realmente importe a tu destinatario y compara con sus intereses, no solo con los tuyos.','I was disappointed; the ending was disappointing; what impressed me was…','Un adjetivo intenso no sustituye una explicación.',
'I found the ending {disappointing}.|disappointed|disappoint',
'What impressed me most {was} the attention to small details.|were|be',
'The plot is fairly predictable, but the characters are convincing.'),
rule('Recomendar para un público','Would recommend + -ing o recommend something to someone permiten aconsejar. Be worth + -ing indica que algo merece el esfuerzo. If you enjoy… delimita el público; even though introduce una concesión. Termina con una recomendación que se desprenda de los criterios anteriores. En conversación, pregunta qué espera la otra persona de una obra antes de asegurar que le encantará.','worth seeing; recommend reading; recommend it to someone','Worth lleva -ing, no to + infinitivo.',
'The exhibition is worth {visiting}.|to visit|visit',
'I would recommend it {to} anyone interested in local history.|for to|at',
'If you enjoy quiet, character-driven stories, this film is likely to appeal to you.')],
'''plot|trama|The plot develops slowly but remains clear.
setting|ambientación|The coastal setting shapes the characters' choices.
narrator|narrador|The narrator does not reveal everything immediately.
character|personaje|Each character has a recognisable motivation.
turning point|punto de inflexión|The letter becomes the turning point in the story.
theme|tema central|One theme is the difference between memory and evidence.
performance|interpretación artística|Her performance makes the conflict believable.
pace|ritmo|The pace becomes faster near the end.
compelling|cautivador|The central question is compelling.
predictable|predecible|The final twist is rather predictable.
thought-provoking|que invita a reflexionar|It is a thought-provoking account of friendship.
moving|conmovedor|The final conversation is moving without being sentimental.
convincing|convincente|The dialogue sounds convincing.
disappointing|decepcionante|The rushed ending is disappointing.
memorable|memorable|One quiet scene is especially memorable.
appeal to|atraer a|The exhibition may appeal to visitors interested in design.
live up to|estar a la altura de|The film does not quite live up to its opening scene.
stand out|destacar|The supporting actor manages to stand out.
be worth|merecer la pena|The first chapter may be worth reading twice.
recommendation|recomendación|A useful recommendation explains who might enjoy the work.''',
'''A film club asked members to write short reviews for people who had not seen the selected film. The first drafts contained enthusiastic adjectives but little explanation. One called the film amazing; another described it as boring. Neither helped a reader decide whether it matched their interests.

The organiser asked each reviewer to choose two criteria and support them with an example that did not reveal the ending. A member who disliked the slow pace acknowledged that the long conversations allowed the characters' relationships to develop. Another, who loved the visual style, admitted that the central mystery was easier to solve than expected.

The revised reviews did not become identical. Their disagreement became more informative. Readers could see whether the reviewer valued suspense, character development or visual atmosphere. The final recommendation could therefore address a particular audience instead of presenting personal taste as a universal judgement.

At the next meeting, members explained how one scene had shaped their interpretation. Discussing the evidence behind their reaction produced a richer conversation than voting on whether the film was simply good or bad.''',
'''Why were the first reviews unhelpful?|They offered judgements without enough reasons.|They contained no opinions.~They explained every criterion clearly.|Los adjetivos no justificaban la valoración.
What did reviewers add?|Criteria and examples without revealing the ending.|Only longer adjectives.~A complete copy of the script.|La evidencia orienta al lector.
Did the revisions eliminate disagreement?|No, they made it more informative.|Yes, everyone chose the same score.~No one read them.|Se clarifican criterios diferentes.
What improved the later discussion?|Explaining how a scene shaped an interpretation.|Only counting votes.~Avoiding all detail.|Se conecta reacción con evidencia.''',
'''A museum visitor called an exhibition confusing because the labels were brief. Another visitor enjoyed discovering connections independently. Their reactions reflected different expectations: one wanted a guided explanation, while the other valued open interpretation.

In a joint review, they described the layout and the limited labels before offering their opinions. They suggested that visitors wanting detailed background might read the introductory guide first. The recommendation became useful when it explained the experience and identified who might benefit from additional support.''',
'''Why did the visitors react differently?|They expected different kinds of guidance.|They visited different museums.~Only one could see the labels.|Se comparan expectativas.
What did the review describe before opinions?|The layout and brief labels.|The visitors' entire biographies.~The final score only.|Se separa observación de valoración.
Who might use the introductory guide?|Visitors wanting detailed background.|Everyone who dislikes reading.~Only the exhibition designer.|El apoyo se dirige a una necesidad.
What made the recommendation useful?|It matched advice to the visitor's expectations.|It promised universal enjoyment.~It removed every limitation.|No se presupone el mismo público.''',
'''I enjoyed the novel mainly because of its narrator. At first, she seems certain about her memories, but small contradictions gradually appear. The pace is slow in the opening chapters, so readers looking for constant action may find it frustrating. However, the details become important later. I would recommend it to people who enjoy discussing how a story is told, rather than only what happens. The ending is worth reflecting on, although I will not reveal it here. If you read it, notice which events the narrator describes confidently and which she avoids explaining.''',
'''What interested the speaker most?|The narrator's way of telling the story.|Constant action.~A simple factual list.|La voz narrativa centra la valoración.
Who might find the opening frustrating?|Readers expecting constant action.|Everyone who enjoys details.~Readers interested in narration only.|Se delimita público.
What should readers notice?|Differences between confident and avoided descriptions.|Only the cover design.~The number of chapters.|Es una estrategia de lectura.''',
'Escribe una reseña de 140–190 palabras de una obra real o inventada. Incluye dos criterios, ejemplos, una limitación y el público recomendado.',
'''The Last Letter is a quiet novel about a young woman who returns to her family home and finds a collection of unfinished messages. Rather than focusing on a series of dramatic events, the story explores how people remember the same experience differently.

The most successful feature is the narrator's voice. Her descriptions seem reliable at first, but small details encourage the reader to question her interpretation. This makes several ordinary conversations surprisingly compelling. The village setting also contributes to the atmosphere without becoming a decorative background.

The opening chapters are slower than necessary, and readers who expect a fast-moving mystery may lose patience. However, many early details become meaningful later, so the slower pace has a purpose even when it occasionally feels excessive.

I would recommend the novel to people who enjoy discussing characters and perspective. It may be less suitable for someone looking for continuous action. If you read it, pay attention to what the narrator leaves unexplained; those gaps are an important part of the experience.''',
'Compara dos obras para una persona con gustos concretos. Pregunta primero qué busca y luego recomienda usando ejemplos y una concesión.',
'''You should see this exhibition. Complete: This exhibition is worth ___.|seeing~visiting|Worth + -ing.
The ending disappointed me. Complete: I found the ending ___.|disappointing|Adjetivo que describe el efecto de la obra.
I advise you to read it. Complete: I recommend ___ it.|reading|Recommend + -ing.
The dialogue impressed me most. Complete: What impressed me most ___ the dialogue.|was|Cleft sentence singular.''',
'''Her ___ was convincing.|PERFORM|performance|Nombre de la interpretación.
The ending was rather ___.|PREDICT|predictable|Adjetivo de algo anticipable.
The scene was especially ___.|MEMORY|memorable|Adjetivo de recuerdo destacado.
The narrator offers a personal ___.|INTERPRET|interpretation|Nombre de la lectura de los hechos.''',
'Expresión oral: usa énfasis para contrastar tu expectativa con tu reacción. Mantén clara la diferencia entre bored y boring al escuchar y producir.')

unit('Correos, solicitudes y reclamaciones','Writing to get things done','✉️','Escribir mensajes con propósito, registro apropiado y peticiones concretas, y revisar su efecto en el lector.',[
rule('Elegir registro y organización','Un mensaje formal necesita un propósito visible, información suficiente y un cierre adecuado. Formal no significa usar palabras largas en cada frase. En un correo informal puedes emplear contracciones y un tono más personal; en una solicitud profesional prioriza claridad y cortesía. Cada párrafo debe cumplir una función: motivo, detalle, petición o siguiente paso. Comprueba que el destinatario sepa qué hacer después de leer.','I am writing to…; I would appreciate…; Please let me know…','El registro depende de la relación y la tarea, no solo del saludo.',
'I am writing {to enquire} about the available dates.|enquire|to enquiring',
'I would appreciate it if you {could send} the details.|can sent|sending',
'Please let me know whether any further information is required.'),
rule('Reclamar con evidencia y solución','Describe el producto o acuerdo, indica qué ocurrió y aporta detalles verificables como fecha o referencia. Explica el efecto y solicita una solución razonable. Evita acusaciones que no puedes sostener. Una reclamación clara permite distinguir un problema de calidad, una expectativa no acordada y un error de comunicación. El lector debe poder investigar sin adivinar qué incidente mencionas.','On [date]…; The confirmation stated…; I would therefore like to request…','Pedir una solución concreta suele ser más útil que expresar solo enfado.',
'The confirmation stated that delivery {was included}.|included it be|was include',
'I would like to request {a refund}.|an refund|refund a',
'Could you explain how you intend to resolve this issue?'),
rule('Solicitar y revisar con propósito','En una candidatura o solicitud, conecta tu experiencia con lo que se pide y apóyala con un ejemplo. En la revisión, comprueba primero contenido y organización, después precisión de formas y puntuación. Un texto puede ser gramaticalmente correcto y no responder a la tarea. Lee la versión final desde el punto de vista del destinatario: ¿qué sabe, qué necesita y qué acción puede tomar?','experience → relevant example → contribution → next step','No copies un modelo completo si cambian destinatario, hechos o propósito.',
'My experience has helped me {develop} strong organisational skills.|developed|developing to',
'I look forward to {hearing} from you.|hear|heard',
'I would be pleased to discuss my application in more detail.')],
'''enquiry|consulta|Thank you for your enquiry about the course.
application|solicitud o candidatura|Please attach your application to the email.
attachment|archivo adjunto|The attachment contains the revised schedule.
reference|número o dato de referencia|Include the booking reference in your message.
confirmation|confirmación|The confirmation states which services are included.
requirement|requisito|The language requirement is described in the notice.
qualification|titulación o cualificación|Explain how your qualification relates to the role.
experience|experiencia|Relevant experience can include voluntary work.
contribution|contribución|Describe the contribution you could make.
availability|disponibilidad|Please confirm your availability for an interview.
inconvenience|inconveniente causado|We apologise for the inconvenience.
resolution|solución de un problema|I would appreciate a prompt resolution.
refund|reembolso|The customer requested a refund.
replacement|sustitución|A replacement could be delivered next week.
regarding|respecto de|I am writing regarding my recent booking.
further|adicional|Please contact me if you need further information.
prompt|rápido y oportuno|Thank you for your prompt reply.
appropriate|adecuado|Choose an appropriate tone for the recipient.
look forward to|esperar con interés|I look forward to hearing from you.
follow up|dar seguimiento|I am writing to follow up on my earlier message.''',
'''A customer wrote a long email after a workshop booking went wrong. The message described frustration in detail but did not include the booking date or explain what solution was wanted. The organiser replied asking for more information, which made the customer feel that the complaint had not been taken seriously.

A friend helped revise the email. They placed the booking reference and date in the first paragraph, explained the difference between the confirmed arrangement and what had happened, and requested either a replacement session or a refund. They removed several accusations about the organiser's motives because those could not be verified.

The revised version was shorter but more complete for its purpose. The organiser could identify the booking, investigate the problem and respond to a specific request. The customer did not need to hide the inconvenience; she explained its practical effect rather than repeating her anger.

The experience showed why good writing involves selecting information for a reader. Accuracy of grammar mattered, but it would not have solved the problem if the essential details and requested action had remained missing.''',
'''What was missing from the first email?|Booking details and a requested solution.|Expressions of frustration.~A description of feelings.|Faltaban datos para actuar.
Why remove claims about motives?|They could not be verified.|They were too polite.~They contained dates.|Se conserva evidencia comprobable.
Why was the shorter version more complete?|It included the information needed to resolve the issue.|It contained more adjectives.~It avoided making any request.|Completo se define por propósito.
What does the case show about good writing?|Grammar is one part of communicating a useful message.|Length guarantees quality.~Feelings must never be mentioned.|Contenido y organización también importan.''',
'''Two applicants described the same volunteer experience. One listed communication, leadership and organisation as strengths. The other explained how she had coordinated six volunteers, clarified their tasks and adjusted the plan when two people were absent.

The second account made the claims easier to assess because the reader could see an action and a result. It did not need to exaggerate the applicant's role. She acknowledged the team's contribution and explained what she had learned. A relevant example turned a general description into evidence the reader could discuss at an interview.''',
'''Why was the second account stronger?|It connected claimed skills with actions.|It used more unrelated qualifications.~It omitted all experience.|El ejemplo aporta evidencia.
What difficulty was handled?|Two volunteers were absent.|No task existed.~An interview was cancelled.|Se explica una adaptación.
How did the applicant describe teamwork?|She acknowledged others' contribution.|She claimed to do everything alone.~She avoided mentioning the team.|No exagera autoría.
What can the reader do with the example?|Discuss it further at interview.|Assume all future work will be perfect.~Ignore relevance.|Permite evaluar y preguntar.''',
'''Thank you for contacting us about your booking. We have located the confirmation and agree that it included a practical session, which did not take place. We can offer a replacement workshop on either the twelfth or the nineteenth. If neither date is suitable, we can arrange a refund of the workshop fee. Please reply with your preferred option and include the original booking reference. You do not need to send the receipt again. We apologise for the inconvenience and will confirm the new arrangement in writing once we have received your reply.''',
'''What problem does the organiser acknowledge?|The included practical session did not happen.|The customer never booked.~No confirmation existed.|La respuesta reconoce el acuerdo incumplido.
What alternatives are offered?|A replacement workshop or a refund.|Only another apology.~A new receipt at extra cost.|Se dan soluciones concretas.
What should the customer include?|The booking reference and preferred option.|The same receipt again.~An unrelated application.|Se pide lo necesario.''',
'Escribe una reclamación o solicitud de 140–190 palabras con destinatario definido, contexto, evidencia, petición y cierre apropiado.',
'''Dear Workshop Coordinator,

I am writing regarding my booking for the photography workshop held on 12 May, reference PH214. The confirmation stated that the fee included both a group introduction and an individual feedback session.

The introduction took place as planned and was useful. However, the individual sessions were cancelled because the tutor had to leave early. We were told that a replacement date would be offered, but I have not yet received any further information.

I understand that unexpected difficulties can arise. Nevertheless, the feedback was an important reason for my booking, and I would still like the opportunity to discuss my work. Could you let me know whether a replacement session will be arranged? If this is not possible, I would appreciate a refund for the part of the workshop that was not provided.

I have attached the original confirmation for reference. Please let me know if you need any additional details to investigate the matter. I look forward to hearing from you.

Yours sincerely,
Alex Rivera''',
'Explica oralmente una reclamación en un minuto. Tu compañero ofrece una alternativa; aclaren condiciones y redacten una confirmación breve.',
'''Please send more information. Complete: I would appreciate it if you ___ more information.|could send|Petición cortés con could.
I hope to receive your reply. Complete: I look forward to ___ from you.|hearing|To preposicional seguido de -ing.
This email concerns my booking. Complete: I am writing ___ my booking.|regarding~about|Introduce el asunto.
Could you tell me if the fee includes materials? Complete: Could you confirm ___ the fee includes materials?|whether~if|Pregunta indirecta sí/no.''',
'''Please confirm your ___.|AVAILABLE|availability|Nombre de disponibilidad.
The email includes an ___.|ATTACH|attachment|Nombre del adjunto.
We apologise for the ___.|CONVENIENT|inconvenience|Nombre negativo del inconveniente.
We hope to find a satisfactory ___.|RESOLVE|resolution|Nombre del resultado de resolver.''',
'Registro oral: practica una petición formal y otra entre amigos conservando la misma información. Ajusta ritmo y tono sin sacrificar claridad.')

unit('Conversar, debatir y sonar claro','Keeping the conversation going','🎙️','Mantener un intercambio espontáneo, cooperar, aclarar y usar ritmo e entonación para comunicar sentido.',[
rule('Turnos y escucha activa','Una conversación no es una sucesión de monólogos. Resume un punto, añade una idea relacionada e invita al otro a responder. Can I add something? permite entrar; What do you think? devuelve el turno. Expresiones breves como I see what you mean muestran seguimiento, pero no equivalen siempre a estar de acuerdo. Si no entiendes, localiza el problema y pide una aclaración concreta.','acknowledge → contribute → invite; Do you mean…?','Repetir automáticamente I agree no demuestra comprensión.',
'I see what you {mean}, but I have a concern about timing.|meaning|means',
'Could you explain what you {mean} by flexible?|do mean it|means',
'Can I add something before we decide?'),
rule('Respuestas breves, eco y confirmación','So do I y neither do I evitan repetir una afirmación, con un auxiliar que corresponde al original. I think so e I hope not recuperan una idea. Las question tags suelen combinar auxiliar y pronombre, con polaridad opuesta; la entonación puede pedir confirmación genuina o buscar acuerdo. En énfasis afirmativo, do/does/did + base puede contrastar una negación anterior. Usa estas herramientas cuando ayuden a la interacción.','I agree → so do I; I cannot → neither can I; You have finished, haven’t you?','El auxiliar depende del verbo y del tiempo, no de una fórmula fija.',
'I have never tried it. — Neither {have I}.|do I|I have',
'You finished the report, {did you not}?|have you not|are you not',
'I do understand your concern; I just disagree about the solution.'),
rule('Inteligibilidad, ritmo y reparación','La claridad depende de contrastes de sonidos, acento de palabra, énfasis de frase, grupos de sentido y entonación. Las formas débiles y los enlaces ayudan a comprender habla conectada, pero no necesitas imitar un acento único. Practica una frase cambiando qué palabra recibe el énfasis y explica cómo cambia el foco. Usa los 44 sonidos para un contraste concreto y vuelve a la frase completa. Si te bloqueas, reformula con palabras conocidas.','thought groups; contrastive stress; weak forms; linking; self-repair','El porcentaje de palabras reconocidas no mide precisión fonética ni competencia B2.',
'If I cannot remember a word, I can {describe} what it means.|described|describing to',
'Let me put that {another} way.|other|the another',
'I meant the earlier train, not the cheaper one.')],
'''turn-taking|alternancia de turnos|Good turn-taking gives both speakers room to contribute.
follow-up question|pregunta de seguimiento|A follow-up question can reveal an important detail.
clarification|aclaración|Ask for clarification when a condition is unclear.
reformulate|reformular|You can reformulate an idea using simpler words.
interrupt|interrumpir|Try not to interrupt before the main point is complete.
acknowledge|reconocer un punto|First acknowledge the concern, then explain your response.
contribute|aportar|Everyone should have a chance to contribute.
hesitation|vacilación|A short hesitation does not prevent clear communication.
fluency|fluidez|Fluency includes connecting ideas and responding appropriately.
accuracy|precisión|Accuracy matters when a small change alters the meaning.
intelligibility|inteligibilidad|Intelligibility is more important than imitating one voice.
stress|acento o énfasis|Sentence stress can signal a contrast.
intonation|entonación|Intonation helps express whether a question is genuine.
rhythm|ritmo|English rhythm varies with the speaker and the message.
chunk|grupo de palabras con sentido|Practise a useful chunk rather than isolated syllables only.
linking|enlace de sonidos|Linking can make a familiar phrase sound different.
weak form|forma débil|A weak form may be difficult to notice at first.
rephrase|reformular|Could you rephrase the last part?
get the point across|hacer entender la idea|A simple example can help you get the point across.
keep the conversation going|mantener la conversación|Ask about the reason to keep the conversation going.''',
'''Two learners prepared a discussion about organising a school event. Both memorised long statements containing impressive vocabulary. During the practice, each waited for the other to finish and then delivered the prepared statement. Their language was often accurate, but they barely responded to each other's ideas.

The teacher gave them a new task: choose one of three event plans while considering a limited budget and an accessibility requirement. Halfway through, she changed one condition. The learners could no longer rely entirely on their scripts. They had to ask questions, compare consequences and explain why a previous suggestion might need to change.

Afterwards, they listened to a recording and marked moments of successful cooperation: a useful follow-up question, a clear summary and a reformulation after a misunderstanding. They also noticed one place where both had spoken at once and missed an important condition.

For the second attempt, they prepared only brief notes and two ways to ask for clarification. The conversation still included pauses and mistakes, but it became more responsive. The improvement lay in using language together to reach a decision, not simply delivering a more polished speech.''',
'''What limited the first discussion?|The speakers barely responded to each other.|They knew no vocabulary.~They asked too many useful questions.|Eran monólogos preparados.
Why change a condition halfway through?|To require adaptation to new information.|To remove the shared goal.~To guarantee no mistakes.|La situación exige interacción real.
What did they identify in the recording?|Examples of cooperation and a missed condition.|Only their speaking speed.~Only the rarest words.|Se evalúa eficacia comunicativa.
What improved in the second attempt?|Responsiveness despite remaining pauses and errors.|Perfect memorisation.~Complete absence of uncertainty.|Fluidez no significa cero pausas.''',
'''In a short exchange, one learner said, "I wanted the BLUE folder," while another repeated the sentence with emphasis on "I." The words were almost identical, but the implied contrast changed. The first corrected the colour; the second could distinguish the speaker's preference from someone else's.

They then practised in a small dialogue so that the emphasis answered an actual misunderstanding. The exercise showed why pronunciation belongs in communication. A sound chart can help with an individual contrast, but the meaning of a whole utterance also depends on stress, grouping and context.''',
'''What does emphasis on BLUE contrast?|The colour.|The speaker's identity.~The date only.|El foco corrige color.
What can emphasis on I contrast?|Whose preference is being expressed.|The spelling of folder.~The size of the room.|El foco recae en la persona.
Why practise the sentence in dialogue?|To make the emphasis respond to a real meaning.|To avoid listening.~To memorise an unrelated rule.|El contexto justifica prosodia.
What limitation of sound charts is noted?|They do not explain every aspect of sentence meaning.|They contain no useful contrasts.~They guarantee fluent discussion.|También cuentan ritmo y contexto.''',
'''For the next discussion, choose a plan together rather than giving two separate speeches. Begin by asking what matters most to your partner. When you disagree, first summarise the point you are responding to. If a word is unavailable, describe the idea and continue. Halfway through, I will give you an extra condition, so listen carefully and be prepared to revise your proposal. Afterwards, identify one successful clarification and one moment where the interaction became difficult. Your aim is a clear, shared decision. Speaking without any pause is not the only sign of an effective conversation.''',
'''What is the main goal?|Reach a shared decision.|Deliver two memorised speeches.~Avoid every question.|La tarea exige cooperación.
What should precede disagreement?|A summary of the point being answered.|An unrelated example.~A change of topic.|Se muestra comprensión antes de responder.
What should learners do if a word is unavailable?|Describe the idea and continue.|Stop the entire task.~Invent an unexplained word.|La reformulación mantiene comunicación.''',
'Escribe 140–190 palabras reflexionando sobre una conversación. Describe un malentendido, una reparación, una estrategia útil y una mejora para repetir la tarea.',
'''During our discussion about the school event, I realised that I had prepared too much of my contribution in advance. I could explain my preferred plan, but I found it difficult to respond when my partner introduced a different priority.

The main misunderstanding concerned the budget. I thought the amount covered only materials, while my partner understood that transport had to be included as well. We clarified this by summarising what each of us had assumed and checking the task instructions together.

One useful strategy was asking a follow-up question before offering another solution. This helped me understand why my partner rejected the first venue. I also used simpler words when I could not remember a particular expression, which allowed the discussion to continue.

Next time, I will prepare key points rather than complete sentences. I also want to leave a short pause after a question so that my partner can finish. Our final decision was clear, but the conversation would have been smoother if I had listened more actively from the beginning.''',
'Debate con una condición sorpresa, luego escucha tu grabación. Evalúa comprensión, respuesta, aclaración y resultado, además de sonidos y gramática.',
'''I enjoy discussion. My partner does too. Complete: I enjoy discussion, and so ___ my partner.|does|Auxiliar de presente simple con tercera persona.
I cannot attend. My friend cannot either. Complete: Neither ___ my friend.|can|Concordancia de respuesta negativa.
Say it in a different way. Complete: Put it ___ way.|another|Another + singular.
You have finished, right? Complete: You have finished, ___ you?|haven't~have not|Tag negativa con auxiliar have.''',
'''The message was easy to understand because of its ___.|CLEAR|clarity|Nombre de la cualidad.
We need to improve spoken ___.|FLUENT|fluency|Nombre de fluidez.
The speaker asked for ___.|CLARIFY|clarification|Nombre de aclaración.
The discussion was genuinely ___.|INTERACT|interactive|Adjetivo de interacción.''',
'Integra sonidos y prosodia: escoge un contraste, practícalo en una palabra y úsalo después en una respuesta espontánea. Busca que se entienda tu intención.')

unit('Proyecto integrador y autonomía','Putting B2 into action','🚀','Integrar lectura, escucha, interacción, escritura y mediación para resolver un proyecto y diseñar el repaso siguiente.',[
rule('Elegir lenguaje según la tarea','Un proyecto completo exige combinar recursos: tiempos para antecedentes y planes, condicionales para alternativas, pasiva para procesos y conectores para justificar. No necesitas exhibir todas las estructuras en cada respuesta. Elige las que aclaren tu intención. Antes de producir, identifica destinatario, propósito, datos y límites. Después comprueba si otra persona puede actuar o responder usando tu mensaje.','purpose → evidence → language choice → clear outcome','La variedad útil depende del sentido, no de insertar estructuras difíciles sin necesidad.',
'If we had checked the figures earlier, we {would have noticed} the error.|would notice yesterday|will have notice',
'By the review date, we {will have collected} feedback from every group.|will collecting|have collect',
'The proposal should be revised once the missing information is available.'),
rule('Revisión integrada','Comprueba primero si respondiste a la tarea y conservaste los datos. Luego revisa orden, conexiones, registro y precisión lingüística. Usa una lista de errores personales en lugar de corregir al azar. Si recibes feedback, reformula la parte problemática y realiza una tarea parecida con información nueva. Copiar la corrección demuestra menos que poder explicar y reutilizar el cambio.','content → organisation → appropriateness → language → transfer','Un porcentaje alto en respuestas cerradas no sustituye una muestra de producción e interacción.',
'The revised report includes the condition {that} was missing from the first version.|what|where',
'Despite {receiving} useful feedback, we still need to test the new version.|receive|to receiving',
'What matters is whether the reader can understand and use the information.'),
rule('Planificar el siguiente ciclo','Una evaluación útil identifica fortalezas y necesidades por habilidad. Revisa lo que recuerdas en días distintos y alterna reconocimiento, recuperación y uso. Mantén un portafolio con un texto inicial, una revisión y una reflexión oral. B2 describe capacidad de comunicarse en tareas variadas; terminar una página no otorga por sí solo ese nivel. Usa evidencias de tareas nuevas y, cuando sea posible, interacción con otra persona para decidir qué practicar después.','attempt → feedback → revision → delayed retrieval → new task','La autoevaluación orienta el estudio; no es una certificación oficial.',
'I look forward to {using} these skills in a new project.|use|used',
'The next practice should focus {on} the difficulties we identified.|in|at',
'We can recognise progress without assuming that every skill is equally strong.')],
'''objective|objetivo|Set an objective that describes something you can do.
evidence|evidencia|Keep evidence of how you used the language.
portfolio|portafolio|Your portfolio can include drafts and reflections.
draft|borrador|The first draft does not need to be perfect.
revision|revisión|A careful revision can improve clarity.
feedback|comentarios para mejorar|Use feedback to plan a specific change.
criterion|criterio|Choose a criterion that matches the task.
accuracy|precisión|Check accuracy when an error changes the message.
range|variedad de recursos|Your range should serve the purpose of the response.
coherence|coherencia|Coherence helps the reader follow the argument.
interaction|interacción|Interaction requires listening and responding.
mediation|mediación comunicativa|Mediation helps another person understand relevant information.
independence|autonomía|Independence grows when you can choose useful strategies.
transfer|transferencia a otra situación|A new task can show transfer of learning.
retention|retención|Delayed practice provides evidence of retention.
reflection|reflexión|A short reflection can identify the next step.
strength|fortaleza|Describe one strength with a concrete example.
priority|prioridad|Choose one priority for the next week.
follow through|llevar algo hasta el final|We need to follow through on the agreed plan.
build on|aprovechar una base previa|The next task should build on what you can already do.''',
'''A learning group completed a course and wanted to know whether the progress charts reflected what they could do outside the exercises. Their tutor organised a project about improving a shared study space. Learners read two short proposals, listened to comments from fictional users and discussed a limited budget.

Each learner then wrote a recommendation for a different recipient. One addressed the centre manager, another wrote to students and a third explained the options to a visitor unfamiliar with the building. The facts were similar, but the selection of detail and register had to change.

The group compared first drafts with revised versions and identified where feedback had improved the message. They also repeated a discussion a week later with one new condition. Some expressions were easy to recognise but difficult to retrieve; some learners wrote clearly yet needed more practice responding to unexpected questions.

The final review did not reduce all this information to a single claim of perfect mastery. It produced a profile of strengths and next steps. Completing the course had given the learners material, practice and evidence. Continuing to use the language in new situations would determine how reliably those skills remained available.''',
'''Why was the project organised?|To compare exercise progress with practical ability.|To replace every learning record.~To award an automatic official certificate.|Se busca transferencia fuera del ejercicio.
Why did recipients differ?|Learners had to adjust detail and register.|The facts had to be invented each time.~No one could read the same language.|Destinatario cambia la selección.
What did the delayed discussion reveal?|Differences in retrieval and response skills.|Permanent perfect mastery.~The uselessness of every exercise.|El desempeño puede variar por habilidad.
What was the outcome of the review?|A profile of strengths and next steps.|One guaranteed level for everyone.~An end to all practice.|Se usa evidencia para continuar.''',
'''One learner obtained a high score in a reading quiz but struggled to explain the article to a partner. Another understood the main point orally but omitted an important condition when writing a summary. Their results were not contradictory: the tasks required related but different skills.

They chose separate follow-up activities. The first practised a short explanation from notes; the second compared summaries with the source to check conditions and qualifications. Both kept the earlier success in their records while addressing the new difficulty. Progress did not need to disappear because a harder task revealed another area to develop.''',
'''Why can the results differ?|Different tasks require different combinations of skills.|Every score is random.~Reading never relates to speaking.|Las habilidades se relacionan sin ser idénticas.
What did the first learner practise?|Explaining from notes.|Only guessing translations.~Avoiding the article.|La práctica responde a su dificultad.
What did the second learner check?|Conditions and qualifications in summaries.|Only handwriting.~Only the title length.|Se recupera información omitida.
Did the new difficulty erase earlier success?|No, both were kept as evidence.|Yes, all progress was deleted.~The text gives no examples.|Una necesidad nueva no invalida un logro.''',
'''For your final project, compare two ways of improving a shared space. Read both proposals and note one strength and one limitation of each. Then listen to a partner's priorities before making your recommendation. Write a clear message for the person responsible for the decision and prepare a short oral explanation for someone who has not read the proposals. After feedback, revise one part of your work and explain why you changed it. Return to the task next week with a different budget. Use that second attempt to decide which language you can retrieve independently and which points need another cycle of practice.''',
'''What must be considered before recommending?|Both proposals and a partner's priorities.|Only the longest proposal.~Only a quiz score.|Se integran fuentes e interacción.
What should follow feedback?|A revision with an explanation of the change.|Deleting all drafts.~Repeating the same text without thought.|La revisión debe ser consciente.
Why repeat with a different budget later?|To check independent retrieval and adaptation.|To guarantee the identical answer.~To avoid using new language.|Combina espaciado y transferencia.''',
'Proyecto final: redacta 180–220 palabras comparando dos propuestas para un espacio compartido. Añade un resumen oral de dos minutos, responde dos preguntas y revisa el texto tras recibir feedback.',
'''Recommendation for the study centre

The centre is considering either extending its opening hours or improving the existing group-work room. Both options could benefit students, but they address different problems. The available feedback suggests that overcrowding during current hours is a more immediate concern than late access.

I therefore recommend testing a revised room layout before committing to longer opening times. Dividing the room into a quiet area and a clearly marked discussion area would help students choose a suitable place for their task. It would also require less additional staffing than keeping the whole building open later.

However, changing the layout would not automatically solve every problem. Noise might still travel between the two areas, and some students may genuinely need evening access. These concerns should be recorded during a six-week trial rather than dismissed.

The centre could monitor how each area is used and collect comments from both daytime and evening students. By the review date, staff would have clearer evidence about demand and practical difficulties. If the trial does not improve access to suitable study space, the longer-hours proposal should be reconsidered.

This recommendation is based on the limited feedback currently available. A wider consultation could change the balance between the two options.''',
'Integra cinco habilidades: lee, escucha, negocia, escribe y explica para otro destinatario. Repite una semana después cambiando presupuesto o prioridad.',
'''We did not check, so we missed it. Complete: If we had checked, we ___ it.|would not have missed~wouldn't have missed|Tercer condicional.
We will finish before the review. Complete: By the review, we ___ .|will have finished|Future perfect.
The feedback helped, but there is more to do. Complete: Although the feedback ___, there is more to do.|helped|Cláusula completa tras although.
We need to practise the difficult points. Complete: We need to focus ___ the difficult points.|on|Focus on.''',
'''The portfolio shows growing ___.|INDEPENDENT|independence|Nombre de autonomía.
The report needs better ___.|ORGANISE|organisation~organization|Se aceptan ambas grafías.
The task requires active ___.|PARTICIPATE|participation|Nombre de participación.
The next step should be chosen ___.|CAREFUL|carefully|Adverbio de modo.''',
'Evaluación oral: comprueba inteligibilidad, grupos de sentido, respuesta al interlocutor y reformulación. Utiliza sonidos aislados para dificultades concretas y vuelve siempre al mensaje completo.')
